  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nMid-level ridging is being flattened across the Central Plains by a \npotent trough moving through the Northern Plains and Great Lakes. \nEnergy embedded in the quasi-zonal flow has helped maintain showers \nand storms into north central Kansas this morning. This activity \nlooks to continue through the morning, potentially through the \nafternoon with weak, but sufficient, elevated instability to \nsupport an isolated storm as well. A surface boundary will \nslowly move north to south across the forecast area today before\nstalling somewhere over east-central or southeast Kansas this \nevening. Temperatures will vary across the forecast area with \nlocations along the KS/NE state line topping out in the 80s \nwhile areas along and south of Interstate 70 will reach the low \nto mid 90s. A strengthening low- level jet and another ejecting \nperturbation will lead to additional showers and storms tonight \ninto Wednesday morning. Highest chances for showers and storms \nwill likely reside north of the forecast area into Nebraska \nalong the nose of the LLJ. However, the passing wave could spark\nsome elevated showers/storms farther south. Coverage and \nlocation of precipitation is uncertain; CAMs depict differing \nsolutions with the 00z HRRR the most aggressive with coverage. \nNonetheless, the environment is not progged to be overly \nconducive to severe weather given 500-750 J/kg of MUCAPE and \nmodest shear around 20kts. \n\nConfidence has increased in showers and storms lingering through at \nleast part of the day Wednesday with the surface boundary holding \nover east-central and southeast Kansas. Wednesday's highs remain \ntricky and will depend on how long precipitation and cloud cover \nlingers. With the recent trend to keep conditions a bit wetter and \ncloudier, highs have been lowered. Expect there to be a tight \ntemperature gradient across the area from the low 70s in \nnorthern Kansas to around 90 degrees south of the I-35 corridor.\nTemperatures may be too warm along and south of I-35, \nespecially if cloud cover holds and prevents solar insolation. \nChances for severe weather have decreased Wednesday \nafternoon/evening since confidence in prolonged destabilization \nhas gone down. Will still need to monitor trends as less clouds \ncould support a marginally more favorable severe weather \nenvironment over portions of east-central Kansas. The LLJ \nincreases again Wednesday night into Thursday morning, but the \nnose of the jet will be situated farther south, a more favorable\nlocation for showers and storms to impact the CWA. Shear and \ninstability could support some strong to severe storms, although\nconfidence is low at this time. Another potential hazard to \nmonitor is locally heavy rainfall as the LLJ pumps moisture \nover the stationary surface boundary with storm motions parallel\nto the boundary. This could lead to training of storms, but \nagain, confidence in this is low.\n\nThe boundary edges farther south on Thursday and skies are progged \nto clear through the day. Some precipitation may hold during the \nmorning, but afternoon sunshine will bump temperatures into the 80s. \nAnother round of storms is likely Thursday night into Friday morning \nas a shortwave dives east-southeast across the Plains. This wave \nfinally shifts the surface boundary away from the local area and \nallows mid-level ridging to build back overhead this weekend into \nearly next week. Consequently, highs will heat back up into at least \nthe mid 90s with dry conditions likely. The NBM remains cooler than \nthe 50th percentile of the GFS, ECMWF, and Canadian ensembles which \nsuggest highs could eclipse 100 degrees across north central Kansas. \nDewpoints will not be as oppressive as the latest heat wave, but \ncould still create heat index values near or slightly above 100 \ndegrees.",
